# Easy Exchange Project Rules

## Source of Truth

* Treat the files in `/specs` as the authoritative source for application requirements.
* Read the relevant specification before implementing or changing functionality.
* Do not add features that are outside the specifications unless the specifications are updated first.
* If specifications conflict or are unclear, identify the conflict before making assumptions.

## Architecture

* Maintain the separation between the React frontend, Express backend, and SQLite database.
* Keep business rules and authorization enforcement on the backend.
* Keep database access within the backend database/data-access layer.
* Keep frontend API communication within the API service layer rather than scattering requests throughout components.

## Security

* Never expose passwords, password hashes, sessions, secrets, or other sensitive information to the client.
* Validate and authorize requests on the server.
* Use parameterized database queries.
* Never commit secrets or credentials to source control.
* Do not weaken security controls simply to make functionality easier to implement.

## Code Quality

* Prefer simple, maintainable solutions over unnecessary complexity.
* Reuse existing components, utilities, and services when appropriate.
* Avoid unnecessary dependencies.
* Do not modify unrelated code while implementing a feature.
* Preserve existing functionality when making changes.

## Testing

* Add or update tests when implementing or changing functionality.
* Tests should verify both successful behavior and important failure/authorization cases.
* Do not modify tests solely to make failing code pass without addressing the underlying behavior.

## Development Process

* Implement functionality incrementally rather than building the entire application in one step.
* Before making significant changes, inspect the existing implementation and relevant specifications.
* After changes, verify that the implementation remains consistent with the product, functional, technical, and UI specifications.
* When a requirement changes, update the appropriate specification before or alongside the implementation.
